# MCP Tools

You have access to these MCPs.

**Rule:** When a relevant MCP exists for the task, **prefer MCP tools over web search**. MCPs provide structured, domain-specific access to docs, schemas, and live APIs. Use web search only when no MCP applies or when the MCP fails.

---

## next-devtools

**Requirements:** Next.js 16+, dev server running (`npm run dev`). The MCP discovers the app via `/_next/mcp`.

### When to Use

| Scenario | Action |
|---------|--------|
| **Error diagnosis** | User reports errors, hydration issues, build failures → use `get_errors` to retrieve current build/runtime/type errors from the dev server |
| **Before implementing changes** | Use `nextjs_index` to discover routes, components, and app structure; understand where to add code |
| **Next.js API questions** | Use `nextjs_docs` for up-to-date docs (e.g. "when to use use client", Server Actions, caching) |
| **Upgrades & migrations** | Help with Next.js 16 upgrade, codemods, breaking changes |
| **Structure audits & compliance** | Use `nextjs_index` to inspect app structure; use `nextjs_docs` for official project structure and file conventions |
| **Page/layout inspection** | Use `get_page_metadata`, `get_project_metadata` for routes, components, rendering info |
| **Server Actions lookup** | Use `get_server_action_by_id` to find source file and function name |

### Key Tools

- `nextjs_index` — Discover running Next.js servers and available MCP tools; call before implementing
- `nextjs_docs` — Fetch Next.js docs by path (read `nextjs-docs://llms-index` resource first for correct paths)
- `nextjs_call` — Execute tools from the running app (get_errors, get_logs, get_page_metadata, etc.)

